= Sensitive topic scope research =

'''Status: Research.''' This page records a proposed BITwiki editorial/security approach for politically and religiously sensitive material. It is not yet binding policy.

== Proposed principle ==

'''Study the system; do not become the conflict feed.'''

Politics and religion are legitimate objects of knowledge. Current partisan campaigning, breaking political news, devotional advocacy, and reputation warfare are not BITwiki editorial products.

This is a scope and security choice, not a claim that those subjects are unimportant.

== Why topic scope is a security issue ==

Knowledge architecture determines what kinds of adversarial attention a system attracts. Risks include coordinated brigading/edit wars, propaganda, harassment or doxxing, state/legal pressure, censorship, search/reputation manipulation, conflict-of-interest editing, volatile claims, governance capture, and agents amplifying contested claims faster than humans can audit them.

These risks should influence review, sourcing, prominence, update cadence, and contributor exposure—not automatically erase legitimate knowledge.

== Durability and lens gate ==

Before BITwiki promotes sensitive-topic material into ordinary canonical navigation, ask:

{| class="wikitable"
! Test !! Question
|-
| Durability || Will this still be useful after the current news cycle?
|-
| Knowledge lens || Is the treatment primarily scientific, historical, philosophical, legal, economic, sociological, systems-oriented, or otherwise analytical?
|-
| Evidence quality || Are claims supported by strong, traceable sources with clear provenance?
|-
| Currentness burden || Does the page require continuous real-time updating to remain accurate?
|-
| Advocacy risk || Is the page explaining a phenomenon, or campaigning for/against a live faction, candidate, movement, or creed?
|-
| Contributor risk || Could editing or attribution materially expose contributors to harassment, legal pressure, or state targeting?
|-
| Harm from prominence || Would creating a prominent portal/category become an attack magnet disproportionate to its durable knowledge value?
|-
| Historical framing || Can a volatile event be represented as a dated case study rather than an always-current feed?
|}

== Politics ==

=== In scope ===

Political science; governance systems; constitutional design; public administration; voting and collective-choice theory; political economy; law/regulatory systems; international relations as scholarship; historical institutions and movements; propaganda/censorship/information warfare as research subjects; comparative government; and systems analysis of states, incentives, power, and coordination.

=== Normally not a product focus ===

Breaking election coverage; daily candidate tracking; partisan scorekeeping; campaign messaging; rapid-response living-politician controversies; unsourced allegations; or pages whose value depends mainly on remaining current with a news cycle.

== Religion ==

=== In scope ===

Religious studies; anthropology/sociology/psychology of religion; philosophy of religion; comparative religion; history, archaeology, textual/manuscript traditions; institutions as social systems; mythology/cosmology as cultural objects; scientifically testable claims where evidence can be evaluated; and the influence of religions on art, law, philosophy, politics, science, and culture.

=== Normally not a product focus ===

Devotional instruction presented as canonical fact; apologetics or anti-religious polemic presented as neutral knowledge; sectarian recruitment; live sectarian advocacy; unsupported supernatural claims represented as established empirical fact.

BITwiki's epistemic machinery should allow a claim or tradition to be documented faithfully without falsely asserting empirical verification.

== Recent events and news ==

BITwiki should not reproduce a continuous news mission. Recent events belong when they materially change a durable knowledge object, provide primary evidence for research, become important historical case studies, or affect BITwiki's own operation/governance/security.

Prefer dated event records and sourced updates over turning a Domain portal into a news feed.

== Sensitive-topic status should not become ontology ==

"Sensitive" is not an Entity type or Domain. If later implemented, it belongs closer to governance/review/risk metadata such as heightened review, living-person sensitivity, current-event volatility, contributor-safety concern, legal/censorship exposure, or conflict-of-interest risk. Do not create properties until recurring workflows demonstrate their concrete jobs.

== Navigation implications ==

BITwiki does not need a prominent top-level Politics or Religion portal merely because general encyclopedias have them. Political science can be navigated through Social sciences / Governance / Systems science; religious studies through Philosophy / History / Anthropology / Psychology / Humanities, with dedicated field portals only if corpus mass warrants them.

Portal prominence is an editorial/security decision as well as a navigation decision.

== Human and agent workflow implications ==

Research candidates include stronger provenance before canonicalization, explicit separation of claims/facts/interpretations/advocacy, datedness/currentness, human review before autonomous publication of highly sensitive current material, minimal contributor identity exposure, reversible audited changes, and anomaly monitoring if open editing becomes significant.

== Policy shape currently favored ==

'''Do not ban the fields. Constrain the mission.'''

BITwiki should be able to deeply study political and religious systems while remaining structurally uninterested in becoming a daily partisan battleground or devotional platform.

